Negative control for the replay-session spec (`R7-4`).

THE DEFECT THIS EXISTS TO CATCH. Replay was cosmetic: playing a track advanced
the steps while the rendered table sat still. The player and the page were never
driving different cells — a filter scene narrowed the rendered rows and a sort
scene reordered them, because criteria travel in the REQUEST. What could not
reach the surface was a scene that WRITES: the create fired, the module
re-fetched, and the stateless resolver answered with the same three recorded
rows it had answered with before. A mutation with nowhere to land is a harness
that only ever demonstrates reads.

This mutant restores exactly that: the answer is still served as recorded, so
every mutation scene still reports success and every step still advances — the
collection simply never moves. That is what makes it the right control. An
assertion that only reads the mutation's own response stays GREEN under it; what
must go red is a read taken AFTER a write, against the same handler list.

The observable, stated so it cannot be satisfied by shape: fire a POST (or a
DELETE) at the handlers, then GET the collection through those same handlers and
compare the rows to the ones the recording started with. Under the original the
created row is on the collection (and the deleted one is gone); under the mutant
the two reads are identical.

Bodies must be injected for this control to say anything: `createForceHandlers`
falls back to the runtime corpus, and an empty handler list matches nothing under
mutant and original alike. Pass the committed recordings in (the spec lane may
read them lawfully — eslint 8h).

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the replay-session case — a write played through the handlers moves the
collection the next read is answered from.

